Edits the manifest — comments preserved — and pushes the edit through
apply's pipeline, restricted to the servers whose rendered
authorized_keys changes. 'revoke USER...' removes each user and every
access entry naming them (offboarding); 'revoke --key KEY...' removes
single keys (a lost laptop): KEY is a SHA256: fingerprint, a public-key
line, or a .pub file path. 'revoke USER... --from-server NAME...' removes only
the users' access to those servers (a selector grant ('*' or @tag) gains an
except: entry); the users and their keys stay. A pair a group grant reaches is
refused; 'revoke USER...' also drops each user from every group. Protected users
(operator: or protected:, and their keys) need --operator; --from-server never
trims an operator. The plan is shown and confirmed once; the
manifest is saved after confirmation and before the first write, so commit and
push it afterwards. --dry-run changes nothing; --no-apply saves the edit
without contacting any server. A server whose only grantee is revoked is
never emptied: the key stays installed there (a warning says so).

Exit codes:
  0  converged: nothing left to do (a header-only update applied, or a server
     a newer manifest wrote whose keys already match); a revoke whose targets
     were already revoked; or, with revoke/rotate --no-apply, the edit is
     saved but not pushed (run the apply hint it prints)
  1  pending changes, unreachable, refused or failed servers (a newer
     manifest's serial, a policy conflict), a write whose login check could
     not prove access (unproven), or a declined prompt; with --sweep, a server
     the sweep could not read
  2  a human must act now: a lockout not restored (the restore of
     authorized_keys or authorized_keys2 after a rejected login check failed,
     or the reconnect after the upload started was refused), a rollback that
     put back a key on the revoked: list, a changed or @revoked host key or a
     changed host key type at the post-write login check (the server's own; a
     bastion's is 1), or revoke/rotate --sweep found a revoked key still
     installed
  3  tool error: bad flags, unloadable manifest, failed preflight, a manifest
     that would keep a policy conflict or add a weak key, a failed serial
     save, or refused before anything was saved or written

Usage:
  sshepherd revoke {USER... [--from-server NAME...] | --key KEY...} [flags]

Examples:
  # Offboard a user everywhere
  sshepherd revoke alice

  # Lost laptop: revoke one key, keep the user
  sshepherd revoke --key ~/alice-laptop.pub

  # Remove a user's access to one server only
  sshepherd revoke alice --from-server db-1

  # Record the edit now, push it later
  sshepherd revoke alice --no-apply

Flags:
      --config string                   path to the manifest (default "sshepherd.yaml")
      --dry-run                         plan and report only; never write
      --from-server NAME                remove the users' access to server NAME only (repeatable); the users and their keys stay
  -h, --help                            help for revoke
      --identity PATH                   private key PATH to authenticate with, in addition to ssh-agent (repeatable; ~/ expands from $HOME)
      --identity-passphrase PATH=FILE   PATH=FILE: passphrase file for one --identity key (repeatable; split at the first =)
      --identity-passphrase-file FILE   FILE holding the default passphrase for any encrypted --identity key
      --json                            emit the plan and outcome as machine-readable JSON on stdout (needs --dry-run or --yes)
      --key KEY                         KEY to revoke: a SHA256: fingerprint, a public-key line, or a .pub file path (repeatable)
      --known-hosts string              path to known_hosts (strict host-key checking) (default "~/.ssh/known_hosts")
      --no-apply                        edit and save the manifest after confirmation; contact no server
      --offer-all-keys                  offer every loaded key to every server (default: only the keys the manifest grants there)
      --operator                        confirm that a target is, or belongs to, a protected user (operator: true or protected: true)
      --parallel int                    max concurrent server connections (default 32)
      --reason string                   free text recorded with each revoked: entry this run adds (printed by every tool; at most 256 bytes)
      --sweep                           after the writes, read every other server and report any that still has a key this run revoked (read-only; exit 2 when one is found; skipped with --dry-run and --no-apply)
      --yes                             skip the confirmation prompt (automation)

Global Flags:
      --color string   colorize output: auto, always, or never (default "auto")
  -q, --quiet          log errors only
  -v, --verbose        enable debug logging
